A||N||5||npavlo||Getrags vs. Isuzu||||5||
Z||000000||npavlo||09-14-2000||09:32 PM||||<BR>ive got a problem with the fact that some of you run izuzu transaxles in your cars.... do they really really hold up?<P>i compared the three transaxles..<BR>getrag 284<BR>getrag/muncie 282/5tm40/nvt550 <BR>isuzu trans<P>and the beafiest one is the getrag 284 out of 3.4L DOHC fwd gms... the isuzu has really wimply thin axles i believe that wont hold up to much power..<BR>those of you who have isuzu transaxles bolted to stock or modified v6s please reply and note your expericances...  can you rip em as hard?  i own a getrag 284 fwd 3.4L dohc car and am planning to swap the engine into a fiero with a getrag 282 transaxle.. i know theryre very similar and the getrag transaxle will hold...  <P>best ive pushed out of my cutlass so far<BR>183hp @5300rpm 187 tq@4000rpm to the wheels...<P>the motor has very few modifications..<BR>i think it would be a good swap for a fiero gt..<P> what you guys htingk?<P>thanks!<P>NicoP<BR><P>------------------<BR>1991 Cutlass Supreme International 3.4L DOHC<BR>5-Speed Getrag 284<BR>Blown Trans, possibly going into fiero --||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-15-2000||01:21 PM||||I think you kind of answered your own question.  I haven't used an Isuzu transaxle with anything except the OE engine (the 4-cylinder), so I really can't offer any hard data.<P>Since you didn't ask  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, I'll offer my opinions, though:<P>1) Generally squeaking  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, the parameter that tells you the "capacity" of a trans. is how much torque it can take (not hp).  <P>2) If I do an engine swap in my 4-cyl, I'm going to use a 4-sp Muncie or 5-sp Getrag.  Reasons: I think GM used the above transaxles with the V6 for reason(s).  Now, if I want a <B>reliable</B> package (and I do!), I'm going to use the higher-capacity trans.<P>If I want a "tinkering toy," and don't mind the idea of swapping transaxles if it fails, later on, I would think about using an Isuzu.<P>3) I seem to read more posts about clutch troubles associated with the Isuzu setup, too.<P>IMHO                      ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||09-15-2000||03:13 PM||||I've heard of guys using the Isuzu trans with V-8s without any problems. I think there may be some info at Archie's site? I've been keeping my eyes open for a getrag, but the Isuzus are far more abundant and cheaper. I don't think there's any question about the Muncies being stronger than the Isuzus, but I think as long as the Isuzu is in good condition to begin with, and not abused, it will hold up to lots of power. I also hear people saying that the Isuzu shifts better than the Muncie. I'm not sure if I want to settle for an Isuzu for my my 4.9 Caddi conversion, or keep looking for a Getrag.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||09-15-2000||03:37 PM||||Look at Archie's FAQ for comments on transmissions, <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/faq.htm." TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/faq.htm.</A>   Maybe Archie will read this thread and add some comments as well.<P>I've got 1 of each tranny, IMO the Isuzu is the smoothest and sweetest-shifting of all 3, followed by the Getrag.  I find the 4-speed  clunky and numb by comparison, especially when shifting into reverse; if you're not careful you'll find yourself in 1st gear instead. <P>If you beat on any of these transmissions you're asking for trouble anyways. <P>One thing to keep in mind  is that you could probably buy at least 5 good-condition Isuzus for the price of 1 Getrag.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||BBBAD GT||09-15-2000||04:15 PM||||I have the Isuzu behind my V8 Fiero, and so far alls well.  Its holding up to 290lb of torque to the wheels without a sweat.  I haven't done a smokey burnout and such yet ( guess I've outlived that part of my life), but I'm sure it would hold up fine.  I do wish the gears were spaced a little farther apart, because I only use first for parking lot antics.  I have a Getag in my 87 GT, and have a hard time telling the two apart with the same shifter (R Dickmans).  Hope this helps.  <P>BTW, I do have the rock crusher munci 4 speed in the basement just incase the Isuzu goes.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Coconut||09-15-2000||05:59 PM||||A couple of months before finding this forum (and learning a few things in the process <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) I was forced to have to replace my Factory Muncie for an Isuzu due to a shortage of "dinero" for a rebuild or settle for a boneyard Isuzu (cheap and in large abundance). As mentioned in an earlier post, the Isuzu does in fact shift much smoother and easier.....but I don't get the pull (especially in 2nd gear) like the Muncie had.<BR>I just wish I had known that the Muncies were that more rare. I would have kept it and had it rebuilt at a later date. Like the song says..."don't it always seem to go....that you don't know what you've got till it's gone". <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Cruisin' On, <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
